 # Cardinality is the bill
 
-<!-- TODO: two or three paragraphs, in this project's voice, on why cardinality is the cost this
-track has not priced yet: everything measured so far is a flow (bytes, work, blocks not read) and
-this one is a *stock* the application chooses. Say what a label with an unbounded value set does to
-a segment's dictionary, to the index's vocabulary and to the identity of an answer, and say why the
-write path cannot notice. Link the pages that carry the neighbouring facts —
-[the segment format](format.md) for the series column, the query language for what a name is asked
-of — and keep it under 200 words. -->
-
-[TODO](TODO)
+Every other cost in this project is a **flow**: bytes written per payload byte, work per query
+phase, blocks a plan did not have to read. Each one answers "how much did that cost", and a
+deployment can watch all of them and still die of the one that is not on the list — how many series
+the store holds. That number is a *stock*, and it is the one the application chooses: a label whose
+values come from a pod name, a user id or a request id turns one metric into a million series, and
+nothing about the traffic says so. Each record is small, each frame is valid, every write succeeds.
+What changed is the shape of everything downstream: the series stream a segment lays its columns
+out by ([the segment format](format.md)), the vocabulary [the sidecar index](../src/engine/index/mod.rs)
+writes down, the identity of every answer, and the grouping a query has to do ([the query
+language](query.md)). This page is the budget: the cap this build ships,
+what a refusal looks like, and what a series costs on this workload, measured.
 
 ## The cap, and the number this build ships
 
-<!-- TODO: the number (`Cap::DEFAULT`, and `series.limit` in the configuration with its four
-layers), and the honest part: the number is *chosen*, so say what it is chosen *for* and point at the
-arithmetic below. Then the rule in two sentences — a series the store already tracks is always
-admitted, a series it has never seen is admitted while there is room — and one paragraph on why a
-frame is judged whole. -->
+**1024 series.** That is `Cap::DEFAULT`, and it is the default of `series.limit` in the daemon's
+configuration ([`src/service/config.rs`](../src/service/config.rs)) — a deployment's number, in the
+same four layers as the queue's bound (a file, `LAKELET_SERIES_LIMIT`, `--series-limit`, and the
+default when nobody says otherwise; `lakeletd --check-config` prints it).
+
+The number is **chosen, not measured**, and this page says so rather than pretending otherwise: it
+is the cardinality that a store of this size can hold at the price measured below, rounded to
+something a person can hold in their head and argue about in a review. What *is* measured is the
+price, and the arithmetic that makes it a budget:
+
+| what | one series, one day |
+| --- | --- |
+| the payload bytes the write path was handed (what the ledger charges) | 167 kB |
+| a segment, for a store that closes one a day | 1 874 B |
+| the sidecar index of that segment | 22 B |
+| a segment, for a store that closes one an hour | 5 350 B |
+| the sidecar index of each of those | 528 B |
+
+So the cap is a budget of about **170 MB a day** at 1024 series on this workload, and — this is the
+number worth arguing about — almost all of it is the log, which this build never truncates
+([a soak is a count of cycles](soak.md)). A deployment that closes segments hourly pays 6 MB a day
+on top of that for the same records, because a segment's dictionary and its frame are paid once per
+segment. Both numbers are the *same* series at the *same* rate; the difference is the flush
+interval, and it is `lakelet/4.20-capacity-model`'s subject rather than this step's.
+
+The rule the cap applies is two sentences long, and both of them are cases in the step's spec:
+
+- **a series the store already tracks is always admitted.** A cap must not break a series that is
+  being reported: a deployment that reached its limit would otherwise start losing the data it
+  already holds, one sample at a time, with no error anywhere;
+- **a series it has never seen is admitted while the ledger has room for it, and refused once it
+  does not.** The refusal is counted, and it is a value — a log line and a client's diagnostics both
+  get the two numbers the decision was made from.
+
+A **frame** is judged as the batch it is, because the write path stores a payload whole or not at all
+(`lakelet/4.5-batch-writes`): a frame whose records name three series is three offers, and a frame
+the cap refuses is a frame the store does not write. Nothing in it is charged.
 
 ## The ledger is derived state
 
-<!-- TODO: where the ledger comes from (the log's records, pruned by the sidecar indexes of the
-segments the store still holds), why it is not a file of its own (the rule `4.14` established for
-derived state), what a missing or damaged index costs instead, and why opening a store is what
-rebuilds it — the restart is the case that decides whether the cap is a policy or a decoration. -->
+Which series the store tracks is not written down in a file of its own, and that is the decision this
+section is about. A store that is wrong about its own cardinality because a sidecar went stale is the
+failure `lakelet/4.14-index-pruning` argued against, so the ledger is rebuilt from the store itself
+whenever the store is opened:
+
+1. **the log**, replayed read-only: every payload the write path accepted. That is where the name of
+   a series whose segment has since been expired still is, and it is why opening a store is what
+   builds the ledger rather than a restart starting the count from zero — a cap that resets on every
+   restart is a cap that leaks as fast as the process manager restarts it;
+2. **the sidecar indexes of the segments the store still holds**: the prune. A series some live index
+   may hold is one the store still has. A series no live index may hold is one it has forgotten —
+   retention expired the segments that carried it — and that is what keeps the cap from being a
+   lifetime quota on a store whose window deliberately forgets.
+
+An index that is **missing or damaged** is a vocabulary nobody can read, and it proves nothing: the
+ledger keeps every name it cannot rule out. That costs tracking *precision* — the store may count a
+series it no longer holds — and never an answer, exactly as a missing index costs a query reads and
+never a record. `lakeletq inspect series --store <DIR>` prints the result: one line per tracked
+series — its name, its records, its bytes, and the first and last millisecond it was seen — in name
+order, or the same objects as one JSON array with `--json`.
 
 ## The three counters, and the identity
 
-<!-- TODO: a row per counter, and the identity `offered == admitted + rejected` with a sentence on
-what crossing it means. Say that the counters count *offers* while `tracked_count` is the live set,
-and show one line of a real transcript. -->
+| counter | what it counts |
+| --- | --- |
+| `series_admitted` | series offers this store took |
+| `series_rejected` | series offers it refused |
+| `tracked_count` | the live set: how many series it holds right now — the number the limit is measured against |
+
+and the identity that ties them together:
+
+```text
+offered == admitted + rejected
+```
+
+Every offer lands on exactly one side, and a series the cap refused is **not** in the ledger. That is
+the line a leak crosses, and it is why the identity is a case rather than a comment: a refused name
+that is inserted anyway leaves `tracked_count` above the limit while the two counters fall short of
+`offered`.
+
+The counters count **offers**, not series — one frame naming eight series is eight offers — so a
+transcript reads like this one, out of the run in the next section:
+
+```text
+series ledger limit=1024 tracked=8 admitted=2880 rejected=0 offered=2880
+```
+
+Eight series, 360 frames, every one of them carrying all eight names: 2880 offers, all admitted, and
+a live set of eight. That is the shape to read: `tracked_count` is the bill, and the other two are
+what the store did with what it was given.
 
 ## What a refused client sees
 
-<!-- TODO: what a client's operator can read — the connection's counters, the one `warn` line per
-refused frame, the `series ledger` line the stop path prints, and `lakeletq inspect series` — and
-what is deliberately *not* visible: the refused records are not sampled, not stored and not
-queryable. The protocol is one-way, so say how a client finds out at all. -->
+Less than it wants to, and the reason is the protocol: ingest is one-way
+(`lakelet/4.1-ingest-protocol` decided that a client's frames are not acknowledged individually), so
+a client cannot be told "this series is over the budget" in a reply. What a client's operator can
+read is:
+
+- **the connection's own counters.** A refused frame is counted into `rejected` beside the frames a
+  queue policy dropped, and the line a connection leaves behind carries the three numbers
+  (`accepted`, `rejected`, `bytes`);
+- **one log line per refused frame**, naming the series and both numbers:
+
+  ```text
+  WARN lakelet::service::ingest: frame refused by the series cap bytes=120 series=cpu,host=h9
+  refusal=refused: this store tracks 1 series and its cap allows 1, so there is no room for a series
+  it has never seen
+  ```
+
+- **the ledger at the stop path**, printed beside the queue's numbers so a run's cardinality is a
+  line an operator reads rather than a dashboard somebody has to build:
+
+  ```text
+  INFO lakeletd: series ledger limit=1024 tracked=8 admitted=2880 rejected=0 offered=2880
+  ```
+
+- **and the store itself**: `lakeletq inspect series --store <DIR>` answers what is tracked and what
+  each series has cost, which is the only way to see the *bill* rather than the refusal.
+
+What is deliberately **not** visible is the refused data itself. The store does not keep a sample of
+what it refused, it does not answer a query about it, and a client that keeps sending is a client
+whose data quietly stops arriving — the refusal is a policy, and the counters are the only part of a
+policy a machine can watch. A deployment that would rather drop *records* than series is a deployment
+that should raise `series.limit` and watch `store_bytes` per series instead; what this store will not
+do is decide that on somebody's behalf.
 
 ## What a series costs, measured
 
-<!-- TODO: the workload you measured, the commands that produced the numbers, and the table. The
-ledger's numbers come from `lakeletq inspect series` on a store a daemon wrote; the segment and index
-numbers come from sealing the same records and reading the two files. Then the arithmetic at the cap:
-what 1024 series cost a day on this workload, and which of the numbers is the one a flush interval
-moves. A page quoting an intention rather than a run is the thing this step exists to prevent. -->
+Two commands against a store of this deployment's own, on the machine this page was written on. The
+workload is eight series, one sample each every fifteen seconds, for one day: 5760 records per
+series, 46 080 records in all.
+
+```sh
+lakeletd --store-dir /tmp/cardinality-store --listen-addr 127.0.0.1:7000 &
+lakelet-load --target 127.0.0.1:7000 --samples 46080 --series 8 --batch 128
+lakeletq inspect series --store /tmp/cardinality-store
+```
+
+```text
+sent=46080 batches=360
+cpu,host=h0 5768 167272 1700000010000 1700046062000
+cpu,host=h1 5874 170346 1700000000000 1700046076000
+cpu,host=h2 5830 169070 1700000011000 1700046068000
+cpu,host=h3 5715 165735 1700000019000 1700046078000
+cpu,host=h4 5825 168925 1700000005000 1700046070000
+cpu,host=h5 5794 168026 1700000002000 1700046061000
+cpu,host=h6 5668 164372 1700000006000 1700046073000
+cpu,host=h7 5606 164014 1700000004000 1700046079000
+```
+
+The eight lines are the whole day: with `--samples 46080 --series 8` the load generator spreads its
+records over the series, so each one gets about 5768 of them, one sample roughly every fifteen
+seconds. The `records` and `bytes` columns are what the write path was handed for that series — the
+payload bytes as they arrived, shared out by record count — and the last two are the first and last
+millisecond the series was seen at. The log the same run left behind is 1 340 645 bytes for
+1 337 760 bytes of payloads, and `admitted=2880` in the ledger line above is 360 frames times eight
+series: the counters count offers.
+
+The disk side of the same records was measured by closing them into segments — the call
+`tests/budget_segments.rs` drives, `Segments::seal` — and reading the two files it leaves:
+
+| shape | segment bytes | index bytes |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| one segment for the whole day | 14 991 | 176 |
+| twenty-four segments, one an hour | 42 796 | 4 224 |
+
+Per series that is 1 874 bytes a day in the first shape and 5 350 in the second, and 22 or 528 bytes
+a day of sidecar index. The index number is the one worth a sentence of its own: a segment's filter
+is a fixed 1024 bits plus a 33-byte zone map and a seal, so it costs the same whether the segment
+holds one series or a thousand — cardinality is not what makes the index expensive, *segment count*
+is. And the segment number is the same lesson as `lakelet/4.8-compaction`: the same records cost
+three times as much when they are closed twenty-four times instead of once.
 
 ## What the cap costs you
 
-<!-- TODO: one paragraph on the price of the policy, which is not a number: the series that were
-refused are the ones nobody can query later, backfill or restore, and what that means on the day of
-an incident. -->
+A cap is not free, and the price is not a number on this page: it is the series that were refused.
+They are not slow, not degraded and not sampled — the store never took them, so there is nothing to
+query later, nothing to backfill from and nothing to restore; the data exists in the client that
+produced it and nowhere else. A deployment that discovers on the day of an incident that the metric
+it needs was refused three weeks ago has lost the incident, not the metric. That is the trade the
+number is: an unbounded store that degrades for everybody, or a bounded one that is honest about
+what it never took and shows exactly which series it is paying for — and the second one is only
+defensible if the first number on this page, the cap, is a decision somebody made on purpose rather
+than a default nobody looked at.
